P(1,'Bharat Our Land','Bharat Our Land: a poem by Subramania Bharati','An ode to India',
 "Imagine standing on a hilltop and shouting how proud you are of your country. Subramania Bharati does exactly this in a poem that praises India's mountains, river, scriptures, warriors, sages and music.",
 [("Bharat Our Land is a poem by Subramania Bharati. It is an ode, a lyrical poem that expresses deep admiration and reverence for a place, here the poet's motherland.","an ode"),
  ("In the first stanza the poet praises three treasures: the mighty Himavant, the generous Ganga and the sacred Upanishads. Each praise ends in a question or a claim that nothing on earth can match it.","three treasures"),
  ("The poet uses symbolism. Himavant stands for strength and permanence, the Ganga for purity and generosity, and the Upanishads for knowledge and a spiritual legacy.","symbolism"),
  ("Calling the Ganga generous is personification, because a river is given a human quality. Its flow is called graceful, which makes the river seem to move like a dancer.","personification"),
  ("The second stanza remembers gallant warriors, sages who sanctified the land, divine music, Brahma-knowledge and the Buddha's dhamma. These are allusions, indirect references the reader recognises from history and tradition.","allusion"),
  ("The refrain, the line that says India is peerless and should be praised, is repeated to build pride and to give the poem a chant-like rhythm.","refrain"),
  ("The poet also uses hyperbole, deliberate exaggeration such as saying there is no equal anywhere on earth. It is not meant literally but to show deep emotion.","hyperbole"),
  ("The hyphens in the opening lines create pauses. The poet states what is ours before the hyphen and makes a strong claim after it. The mood is proud and joyful and the tone is admiring.","hyphens, mood and tone")],
 ("Identify the device in each case: calling the Ganga generous, saying there is no equal anywhere on earth, and repeating the praise line.",[("Giving a river a human quality is personification.","Human trait on a non-human thing."),("Saying nothing on earth equals the mountain is hyperbole.","Deliberate exaggeration."),("The repeated praise line is the refrain.","A line that returns.")]),
 ("Explain how the poet builds national pride using symbols, allusions and the refrain.",["Himavant, Ganga and Upanishads are symbols","Allusions to sages, warriors and the Buddha","The refrain repeats the praise"],["Thinking hyperbole must be literally true"]),
 ["Bharat Our Land is an ode by Subramania Bharati praising India.","The Himavant, Ganga and Upanishads are symbols of strength, purity and wisdom.","Personification, hyperbole, allusion, the refrain and hyphens give the poem its feeling.","The mood is pride and the tone is admiring."],
 [mcq("p1-1",1,"A lyrical poem that expresses deep admiration for a person, place or idea is called","an ode",["a riddle","a news report"],"An ode praises its subject with enthusiasm."),
  mcq("p1-2",1,"In the poem the Himavant symbolises","strength and permanence",["purity and generosity","sadness and loss"],"The mighty mountain stands for strength and permanence."),
  tf("p1-3",1,"Calling the Ganga generous is an example of personification.",True,"A human quality is given to a river."),
  mcq("p1-4",1,"A line that is repeated through a poem is a","refrain",["footnote","title"],"The refrain builds pride and a chant-like rhythm."),
  mcq("p1-5",2,"The reference to the Buddha's dhamma is an example of","allusion",["onomatopoeia","simile"],"An allusion is an indirect reference to a well-known person or idea."),
  mcq("p1-6",2,"Saying there is no equal anywhere on earth is","hyperbole",["a pun","an understatement"],"It is a deliberate exaggeration to show deep emotion."),
  tf("p1-7",2,"Hyperbole must be taken literally.",False,"It is exaggeration for effect, not a literal claim."),
  short("p1-8",3,"Name the poet of Bharat Our Land.",["subramania bharati","bharati","subramanya bharati"],"The poem is by Subramania Bharati.")])

P(2,'Gifts of Grace: Honouring Our Vocations','Gifts of Grace: Honouring Our Vocations','The dignity of every kind of work',
 "Think of everyone whose work you used today: the person who made your shoes, the electrician who keeps the lights on, the cook who made lunch. This poem listens to all of them celebrating together.",
 [("Gifts of Grace: Honouring Our Vocations is an anonymous poem. The speaker hears Bharat celebrating the many vocations of its people.","the speaker hears Bharat"),
  ("The poem names craftspeople, artisans with lutes, carpenters, electricians, boatmen, shoemakers, a cook, a designer and a mason, so it gives us a whole community at work.","many vocations"),
  ("Each worker gets one vivid detail: carpenters work with mathematical precision, electricians bring light with wires, and boatmen return with stories of the sea.","one detail for each"),
  ("The poem uses auditory imagery, language that appeals to hearing, with words such as hear, humming, singing and rhythm. We seem to hear the work.","auditory imagery"),
  ("Shoemakers affirm the quality of their work for the feet that walk, dance, run and jump. The poem shows that humble work supports everything we do.","honouring humble work"),
  ("The poem begins and ends with the speaker hearing Bharat celebrating the varied voices. This framing gives it a feeling of completeness.","framing lines"),
  ("The last lines say that the voice of a vocation is the voice of a person's identity. What we do is a part of who we are.","vocation and identity"),
  ("The poem does not follow a strict rhyme scheme, so it is free verse. Its theme is the dignity of labour and respect for every vocation.","free verse, dignity of labour")],
 ("Explain why the poet says the voice of a vocation is the voice of identity.",[("Each worker is proud of the skill that belongs to them.","Look at celebrating what belongs to them."),("A person's work shapes how they are known.","Work builds identity."),("So respecting work means respecting the person.","Dignity of labour.")]),
 ("Why does the poet list so many different vocations?",["To show every kind of work has value","Auditory imagery lets us hear the work","Work is part of identity"],["Thinking only skilled or famous jobs are celebrated"]),
 ["The poem is an anonymous celebration of the varied vocations of Bharat.","Auditory imagery lets the reader hear the work.","The poem begins and ends with the same idea and has no strict rhyme scheme.","Its theme is the dignity of labour and identity through work."],
 [mcq("p2-1",1,"The poem celebrates","the varied vocations of Bharat",["a battle","a festival of lights only"],"The speaker hears Bharat celebrating different vocations."),
  mcq("p2-2",1,"The carpenters are admired for working with","mathematical precision",["great speed only","expensive tools"],"They create anything from wood with mathematical precision."),
  tf("p2-3",1,"The poem follows a strict rhyme scheme.",False,"It is free verse with no strict rhyme scheme."),
  mcq("p2-4",1,"Words such as hear, humming and singing create","auditory imagery",["visual imagery only","no imagery"],"They appeal to the sense of hearing."),
  mcq("p2-5",2,"The shoemakers affirm the quality of their work for the feet that","walk, dance, run and jump",["only march","rest"],"The poem lists all the ways we use our feet."),
  mcq("p2-6",2,"The last lines say the voice of a vocation is the voice of","identity",["silence","money"],"Work is a part of who we are."),
  tf("p2-7",2,"The poem shows that only some vocations deserve respect.",False,"Every vocation is celebrated."),
  short("p2-8",3,"Name the main theme of the poem in two or three words.",["dignity of labour","dignity of work","respect for work","honouring vocations"],"The poem honours all work.")])

P(3,'Canvas of Soil','Canvas of Soil: a poem by Maya Anthony','A garden painted by gardeners',
 "A painter chooses colours, brushes and a canvas. This poem says a gardener does the same, but the colours are seeds and the canvas is the earth.",
 [("Canvas of Soil is a short poem by Maya Anthony that compares a garden to a painting.","garden as painting"),
  ("The earth is called a palette, rich and deep, and the seeds planted by gardeners are called brushstrokes. These are metaphors because one thing is described directly as another.","metaphors"),
  ("As spring comes, blossoms open like a painted sight dancing in morning light. The poem gives flowers the lively movement of dancers.","personification of blossoms"),
  ("Shades of green, red and blue make nature's artwork, always new. This is visual imagery, language that helps us see colours.","visual imagery"),
  ("Each plot is called a canvas, so every small piece of ground becomes a space for creation where art and life meet.","art and life coincide"),
  ("The poem has six rhyming couplets, pairs of lines whose end words rhyme, such as deep and seep, and wide and coincide. The rhyme gives it a light, musical flow.","rhyming couplets"),
  ("In the final lines the gardens made by the hands of those who till become paintings that stay still, a picture frozen in time.","paintings that stay"),
  ("The mood is cheerful and peaceful and the theme is that gardening is an art that joins nature and human creativity.","mood and theme")],
 ("Pick out one metaphor and explain what is being compared.",[("The earth is called a palette.","Soil is compared to a painter's palette."),("The soil holds the material for the garden as a palette holds paint.","Shared idea: the source of colour."),("So gardening is shown as painting.","Art and nature combined.")]),
 ("How does the poet turn gardening into an art?",["Earth is a palette, seeds are brushstrokes","Plots are canvases","Rhyming couplets and imagery add beauty"],["Thinking the poem is about a real painter"]),
 ["Canvas of Soil compares a garden to a painting.","Metaphors: earth is a palette and seeds are brushstrokes.","The poem has rhyming couplets and vivid visual imagery.","The theme is that gardening is an art joining nature and creativity."],
 [mcq("p3-1",1,"Who wrote Canvas of Soil?","Maya Anthony",["Subramania Bharati","Rabindranath Tagore"],"The poem is by Maya Anthony."),
  mcq("p3-2",1,"In the poem the seeds are compared to","brushstrokes",["clouds","coins"],"The seeds are brushstrokes on the earth's canvas."),
  tf("p3-3",1,"The poem compares a garden to a painting.",True,"That comparison runs through the whole poem."),
  mcq("p3-4",1,"A pair of rhyming lines is called a","couplet",["refrain","stanza break"],"The poem is made of rhyming couplets."),
  mcq("p3-5",2,"Describing earth directly as a palette is a","metaphor",["simile","pun"],"A metaphor states one thing is another."),
  mcq("p3-6",2,"The phrase shades of green, red and blue is an example of","visual imagery",["auditory imagery","hyperbole"],"It helps us see colours."),
  tf("p3-7",2,"The poem has a sad mood.",False,"The mood is cheerful and peaceful."),
  short("p3-8",3,"Name the art form to which gardening is compared.",["painting","art","a painting"],"The garden is compared to a painting.")])

P(4,'I Cannot Remember My Mother','I Cannot Remember My Mother: a poem by Rabindranath Tagore','Memories carried by the senses',
 "You may forget a face but still recognise a song or a smell from long ago. In this poem a child who cannot remember his mother still finds her everywhere.",
 [("I Cannot Remember My Mother is a poem by Rabindranath Tagore in which the speaker says he has no clear memory of his mother.","the speaker's loss"),
  ("Each of the three stanzas begins with the same line, which is a refrain. It stresses the sadness of not remembering.","refrain"),
  ("In the first stanza a tune seems to hover over his playthings, the tune of a song she hummed while rocking his cradle. This is auditory imagery.","sound memory"),
  ("In the second stanza the smell of shiuli flowers on an autumn morning brings the scent of the temple's morning service, and he feels it as the scent of his mother. This is olfactory imagery, about smell.","smell memory"),
  ("In the third stanza, looking into the blue distance from his window, he feels his mother's gaze has spread all over the sky. This is visual imagery and a metaphor that makes the sky a comforting presence.","sight memory"),
  ("So the three stanzas move through three senses: hearing, smell and sight. The mother is felt in ordinary moments of play, morning and sky.","three senses"),
  ("The poem is written in free verse, without a regular rhyme scheme, in simple conversational language.","free verse"),
  ("The mood is tender and longing, and the theme is that love lives on in everyday things even when direct memory is lost.","mood and theme")],
 ("Match each stanza with the sense it uses.",[("Stanza 1 uses the tune he hears while playing.","Hearing."),("Stanza 2 uses the smell of shiuli flowers.","Smell."),("Stanza 3 uses the blue sky and her gaze.","Sight.")]),
 ("How does the poet show his mother's presence although he cannot remember her?",["Tune, scent and sky bring her back","The refrain stresses not remembering","The mood is tender longing"],["Thinking he remembers her clearly"]),
 ["The poem by Rabindranath Tagore is about a child who cannot remember his mother but feels her presence.","The refrain opens each of the three stanzas.","It uses hearing, smell and sight imagery.","The mood is tender and longing."],
 [mcq("p4-1",1,"The poem is written by","Rabindranath Tagore",["Charles Swain","David Roth"],"Tagore wrote this poem."),
  tf("p4-2",1,"The speaker remembers his mother clearly.",False,"He says he cannot remember her."),
  mcq("p4-3",1,"The line that begins every stanza is a","refrain",["couplet","riddle"],"It is repeated to stress the feeling."),
  mcq("p4-4",1,"The smell of shiuli flowers reminds him of","the temple's morning service and his mother",["a market","the sea"],"The scent comes as the scent of his mother."),
  mcq("p4-5",2,"The tune hovering over his playthings is","auditory imagery",["olfactory imagery","hyperbole"],"It appeals to the sense of hearing."),
  mcq("p4-6",2,"In the third stanza he feels his mother's gaze","has spread all over the sky",["is in a mirror","is in a book"],"The sky becomes her comforting presence."),
  tf("p4-7",2,"The poem is full of loud, angry language.",False,"The mood is tender and longing."),
  short("p4-8",3,"Name one sense, other than sight, that the poem uses.",["hearing","smell","sound"],"The poem uses hearing and smell as well as sight.")])

P(5,'Nine Gold Medals','Nine Gold Medals: a poem by David Roth','When a race becomes a lesson in kindness',
 "Races are usually about who comes first. This poem tells a true-style story of a race where something more important than winning happened.",
 [("Nine Gold Medals is a narrative poem by David Roth. A narrative poem tells a story, and this one has eight stanzas.","narrative poem"),
  ("Athletes from all over the country have trained for weeks and months to race in a hundred-yard dash. The spectators gather and excitement is high.","the setting"),
  ("Nine resolved athletes wait at the starting blocks. When the pistol fires, the runners charge ahead.","the start"),
  ("The smallest runner stumbles, falls to the asphalt and cries out in frustration, because his dreams and efforts seem dashed.","the fall"),
  ("The other eight runners stop, turn back and help him to his feet. This is the turning point of the poem.","the turning point"),
  ("All nine then join hands and walk to the finish line. A banner above reads Special Olympics, which fits the spirit of the moment.","hands joined"),
  ("The race ends with nine gold medals, a standing ovation and nine beaming faces. The poet says those faces say more than words can.","nine gold medals"),
  ("The theme is that compassion and teamwork matter more than winning. The mood moves from excitement to anxiety to joy.","theme and mood")],
 ("Describe how the mood changes in the poem.",[("It starts with excitement as the race begins.","Anticipation."),("It turns anxious when the smallest runner falls.","Worry and sadness."),("It ends in joy when all finish together.","Warmth and pride.")]),
 ("Why are the medals called gold for all nine runners?",["All finished together","Kindness mattered more than winning","The event was the Special Olympics"],["Thinking one runner won gold"]),
 ["Nine Gold Medals is a narrative poem by David Roth in eight stanzas.","The smallest runner falls and the other eight turn back to help him.","All nine finish together hand in hand.","Compassion and teamwork matter more than winning."],
 [mcq("p5-1",1,"A poem that tells a story is called","a narrative poem",["a riddle","an ode"],"Nine Gold Medals tells a story."),
  tf("p5-2",1,"The smallest runner stumbled and fell.",True,"He fell to the asphalt."),
  mcq("p5-3",1,"The other eight runners","turned back to help him",["ran on and won","left the field"],"They helped him to his feet."),
  mcq("p5-4",1,"The banner above the runners mentioned","Special Olympics",["World Cup","Asian Games"],"It matched the spirit of the moment."),
  mcq("p5-5",2,"The turning point of the poem is when","the eight runners turn back",["the pistol fires","the crowd arrives"],"Their act changes the whole race."),
  mcq("p5-6",2,"The main theme of the poem is","compassion matters more than winning",["speed is everything","rules are boring"],"The runners choose kindness over a win."),
  tf("p5-7",2,"The poem ends with only one winner.",False,"All nine finish together and win nine gold medals."),
  short("p5-8",3,"Name the poet of Nine Gold Medals.",["david roth","roth"],"The poem is by David Roth.")])

P(6,'A Friend Found in Music','A Friend Found in Music: a poem by Bryanna T. Perkins','When music keeps you company',
 "Some friends never speak yet always seem to understand. For the poet, that friend is music.",
 [("A Friend Found in Music is a short poem by Bryanna T. Perkins about what music means to the speaker.","music and the speaker"),
  ("It begins with metaphors. Music is described as the ocean that pulls the speaker to the shore and as the rhythm that moves the speaker to the core.","metaphors"),
  ("The line pattern repeats the words music is again and again. This repetition at the start of lines is called anaphora.","anaphora"),
  ("Music is also called therapy for the blues. When the speaker feels low, music lifts the spirits and helps them pull through.","music as therapy"),
  ("When the speaker is cheerful, music was there too, so music accompanies both joy and sorrow.","joy and sorrow"),
  ("The last lines call music the needed friend when no one seems to care. Music becomes a companion in loneliness.","music as a friend"),
  ("The poem has an easy, musical rhyme: the second and fourth lines of each stanza rhyme, such as blue and through, and there and care.","rhyme"),
  ("The mood is hopeful and comforting, and the theme is that music supports us emotionally in every mood.","mood and theme")],
 ("Show how repetition and rhyme help the poem sound like music.",[("The words music is begin many lines.","Anaphora."),("Blue rhymes with through and there with care.","End rhyme."),("Together they give a regular, song-like beat.","Sound supports meaning.")]),
 ("Why does the poet call music a friend?",["It comforts in sad times","It is with us in happy times","It is there when no one seems to care"],["Thinking music only helps in happy moments"]),
 ["The poem by Bryanna T. Perkins shows music as a friend and therapy.","It uses metaphors and anaphora.","Alternate lines rhyme, giving a song-like beat.","The mood is hopeful and comforting."],
 [mcq("p6-1",1,"In the poem music is compared to","the ocean",["a desert","a mountain"],"Music is the ocean that pulls the speaker to the shore."),
  tf("p6-2",1,"Music helps the speaker only when cheerful.",False,"It also helps when the speaker feels blue."),
  mcq("p6-3",1,"Repeating the words music is at the start of lines is called","anaphora",["rhyme","allusion"],"Repeating a word at the start of lines is anaphora."),
  mcq("p6-4",1,"Music is called the needed friend when","no one seems to care",["it is raining","it is morning"],"Music keeps company in loneliness."),
  mcq("p6-5",2,"The rhyme of blue and through is","end rhyme",["alliteration","onomatopoeia"],"The rhyming words come at line ends."),
  mcq("p6-6",2,"The mood of the poem is","hopeful and comforting",["angry","fearful"],"Music lifts the spirits."),
  tf("p6-7",2,"The poem uses metaphors for music.",True,"Music is called the ocean, rhythm, therapy and a friend."),
  short("p6-8",3,"Name the poet of A Friend Found in Music.",["bryanna t. perkins","bryanna perkins","perkins"],"The poem is by Bryanna T. Perkins.")])

P(7,'Words','Words: a poem by Charles Swain','Few true words and many empty ones',
 "Have you ever heard someone talk for ten minutes and say nothing? Charles Swain says words alone cannot satisfy the heart, and tells us why.",
 [("Words is a poem by Charles Swain. Its six stanzas of four lines each rhyme in an ABAB pattern, so the first and third lines rhyme and the second and fourth lines rhyme.","six stanzas, ABAB"),
  ("The poet argues that words alone cannot satisfy the heart. If they could, the heart would have fewer cares.","words cannot satisfy the heart"),
  ("He compares words to summer birds that fly away and leave only empty air. This is a simile because it uses like or a comparison.","simile: summer birds"),
  ("The heart is called a pilgrim upon earth. This is a metaphor that shows the heart searching for something meaningful, and finding that many words are as worthless as weeds.","metaphor and weeds"),
  ("A little said and truly said gives deeper joy than a host of words that reach the head but never touch the heart. Sincere words carry more value than many words.","quality over quantity"),
  ("The voice that cheers a lonely home often has the fewest words to say, but those few are dear.","few, dear words"),
  ("Later words are compared to plants that make a gaudy show, all blossom to the root but give no fruit. Showy speech with no substance gives nothing.","showy plants, no fruit"),
  ("The tone is gentle and thoughtful, and the theme is that sincere words matter more than many words.","tone and theme")],
 ("Explain the comparison of words with plants that blossom but give no fruit.",[("The plants make a bright, showy display.","Gaudy show."),("But they never produce fruit.","No lasting value."),("So showy speech without sincerity gives no real benefit.","Substance over show.")]),
 ("What does the poet suggest about speaking a lot and speaking sincerely?",["Few sincere words reach the heart","Many empty words only reach the head","Showy speech gives no fruit"],["Thinking more words are always better"]),
 ["Words is a poem by Charles Swain in six ABAB stanzas.","Words are compared to summer birds and showy plants.","The heart is a pilgrim, a metaphor.","Sincere words matter more than many words."],
 [mcq("p7-1",1,"The poet of Words is","Charles Swain",["Dorothy Hewitt","Maya Anthony"],"The poem is by Charles Swain."),
  mcq("p7-2",1,"Words are compared to","summer birds",["rivers","mountains"],"They depart and leave empty air."),
  tf("p7-3",1,"The poet says many words always satisfy the heart.",False,"He says words alone cannot satisfy the heart."),
  mcq("p7-4",1,"Calling the heart a pilgrim is a","metaphor",["onomatopoeia","pun"],"The heart is directly described as a pilgrim."),
  mcq("p7-5",2,"The rhyme scheme of each stanza is","ABAB",["AABB","ABCD"],"Lines one and three rhyme, and lines two and four rhyme."),
  mcq("p7-6",2,"Plants that are all blossom but give no fruit represent","showy words without substance",["honest speech","silence"],"They look impressive but give nothing."),
  tf("p7-7",2,"The poet values a few sincere words.",True,"A little said and truly said gives deeper joy."),
  short("p7-8",3,"Does the poet prefer many words or few true words?",["few true words","few","few words","few sincere words"],"He prefers a few truly said words.")])

P(8,'Believe in Yourself','Always Believe in Yourself: a poem by Dorothy Hewitt','A poem that speaks to you directly',
 "Imagine a friend putting a hand on your shoulder and telling you that you have everything you need. This poem does that.",
 [("Believe in Yourself is a poem by Dorothy Hewitt. It speaks directly to the reader in the form of advice.","direct advice"),
  ("The opening instruction is to always believe in yourself, not to limit yourself, and to be kind to yourself.","believe, don't limit"),
  ("The poem repeats the word always. This repetition emphasises that confidence must be steady.","repetition of always"),
  ("It reminds us that we already have the intelligence and ability we need, so we can reach what we are after, even if it does not come the way we expect.","you have what you need"),
  ("When we are tested beyond our endurance, the poem advises us to continue, persist and hold on to courage.","persist"),
  ("It asks us to let laughter and encouragement surround us, so a positive circle of people helps us grow.","encouragement"),
  ("The last lines advise thinking big and keeping hands and heart open so we can receive all of life's gifts. Open hands and heart are a metaphor for being ready for life.","open hands and heart"),
  ("The poem is in free verse and uses the imperative, a form of verb that gives a command or advice. The tone is encouraging and the theme is self-belief with persistence.","free verse and tone")],
 ("Pick out the imperative advice in the poem and say what it teaches.",[("Always believe in yourself.","Self-belief."),("Continue and persist when tested.","Perseverance."),("Keep hands and heart open.","Openness to opportunity.")]),
 ("How does the poem help a reader who feels like giving up?",["You already have the ability you need","Persist and hold on to courage","Encouragement and an open heart bring gifts"],["Thinking confidence means never failing"]),
 ["Believe in Yourself is an encouraging poem by Dorothy Hewitt.","It uses imperative lines and repetition.","It advises persistence, courage and an open heart.","Its theme is self-belief with perseverance."],
 [mcq("p8-1",1,"The poem is written by","Dorothy Hewitt",["Charles Swain","Rabindranath Tagore"],"The poem is by Dorothy Hewitt."),
  tf("p8-2",1,"The poem advises us to limit ourselves.",False,"It says do not limit yourself."),
  mcq("p8-3",1,"When tested beyond endurance the poem advises","continue and persist",["give up","blame others"],"Persistence and courage are advised."),
  mcq("p8-4",1,"The tone of the poem is","encouraging",["mocking","angry"],"It is advice from a caring voice."),
  mcq("p8-5",2,"Keeping hands and heart open is a","metaphor for being ready for life's gifts",["literal handshake","rule of grammar"],"It means staying ready to receive opportunity."),
  mcq("p8-6",2,"Commands such as believe and continue are called","imperatives",["questions","exclamations only"],"Imperatives give advice or commands."),
  tf("p8-7",2,"The poem says you already have the ability you need.",True,"It says you have the intelligence and ability needed."),
  short("p8-8",3,"Name the word repeated at the start of several lines.",["always"],"The word always is repeated.")])
print('done')
